= The long-term impacts of opioid use before and after joint replacement surgery: Report on finding the optimal matching model
#smallcaps[Published]\ 27 July 2021

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Pryymachenko Y, Wilson R, Abbott JH. The long-term impacts of opioid use before and after joint replacement surgery: Report on finding the optimal matching model. OUR Archive, 27 July 2021. Available at: https://hdl.handle.net/10523/12166", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
Total joint replacement (TJR) is one of the most common elective surgical
procedures globally. While this surgery should provide patients with substantial,
if not complete, pain relief from end-stage arthritis, post-surgical pain remains
common. Opioids are part of the acute pain management strategy post-TJR. Long-term
opioid use is not recommended due to substantial risk of harm, the diversion of
prescription opioids for non-medical use, and the risk of overdose, injection-related
harm, and dependence. However, use of opioids beyond the immediate post-surgical
recovery period of TJR is widespread (e.g., 29 percent of hip replacement patients
and 36 percent of knee replacement patients are prescribed opioids 3-12 months
after surgery in New Zealand; similar numbers have also been reported in Australia
and higher in the United States).

One of the strongest risk factors of persistent
postoperative opioid use is pre-and perioperative opioid use, which is also associated
with several other adverse outcomes such as reduced pain relief following surgery,
increased risk of surgical complications and early revision. However, there are
no studies to date that look at broader economic, social, and healthcare outcomes
of pre- and perioperative opioid use, either manifesting directly or through its
effect on long-term opioid use.

This project aims to identify the medium- to
long-term health, economic, and social outcomes of pre- and perioperative opioid
use in patients undergoing total hip and knee replacement surgery in New Zealand
using the matching approach. The current document reports on the procedure and
results of the first stage of the matching process – finding the optimal matching
model. Performing and reporting on the matching procedure before undertaking outcome
analysis is done to minimize the risk of bias due to low quality matching or selective
reporting of models favourable to the expected outcomes. The analysis was pre-specified
and published on the Open Science Framework.

